<h1>About</h1>
<p>This site tells you which version of TLS your browser used to connect to it.
Load the <a href="/">home page</a> and it shows you. If you want the answer as
data instead, there's a JSON endpoint for that.</p>
<p>TLS is what encrypts the connection between your browser and the websites you
visit. The versions in use today are <code>TLS 1.3</code> and
<code>TLS 1.2</code>. <code>TLS 1.0</code> and <code>TLS 1.1</code> are old and
deprecated, and you don't want to still be on them.</p>

<h2>The JSON API</h2>
<p>Request <a href="/v1/version.json"><code>/v1/version.json</code></a> and you
get back the version:</p>
<pre><code>$ curl https://tlsversion.com/v1/version.json
{"version":"TLS 1.3"}</code></pre>
<p>One field, <code>version</code>. That's the whole response, and it's meant to
stay that way, so you can build against that shape without worrying it'll
change.</p>

<h2>Why not howsmyssl.com?</h2>
<p>This site comes from the same person who runs
<a href="https://www.howsmyssl.com">howsmyssl.com</a>. Its
<a href="https://www.howsmyssl.com/a/check"><code>/a/check</code></a> API hands
back a big report about your connection: the cipher suites, a security rating,
all of it. But plenty of people were calling it only to pull one field,
<code>tls_version</code>, out of that report and ignore the rest. Producing the
rest isn't free.</p>
<p>So this is the small version of that. If the TLS version is all you're after,
use this. If you want the full picture of a connection, howsmyssl.com is still
there.</p>

<p><a href="/">← Check my version</a></p>
